("48","Pythonic Thinking","Write code that follows Python's strengths: clear iteration, unpacking, comprehensions, context managers, and readable data transformations.","Pythonic code is not about clever one-liners; it is about expressing intent clearly.","Rewrite a nested loop using enumerate or a comprehension where it genuinely improves clarity.","Over-compressing code until nobody can read it.","Compare a compact solution with a clear multi-line solution and choose deliberately."),
("49","Advanced Function Patterns","Explore positional and keyword arguments, *args, **kwargs, keyword-only parameters, and small functional patterns.","Good function interfaces make code easier to reuse and test.","Build a function that accepts a required name plus optional keyword settings.","Using *args and **kwargs when explicit parameters would be clearer.","Design a function API for a small logging helper."),
("50","Scope, LEGB & Closures","Understand local, enclosing, global, and built-in name lookup and how closures retain state.","Scope explains many surprising Python behaviors.","Create a closure that remembers a counter between calls.","Using global state when a small object or closure would be safer.","Trace where Python finds three different variable names."),
("51","Dataclasses","Use dataclasses to model structured records with readable defaults, generated methods, and type annotations.","They remove repetitive boilerplate from many data-heavy classes.","Create a Product dataclass with validation-friendly fields.","Putting complex business logic into a data container without a reason.","Convert an ordinary record class into a dataclass and compare it."),
("52","Enums & Constants","Use Enum for named states and constants instead of scattering magic strings or numbers through a program.","Named states make code and validation easier to understand.","Create a Status enum with pending, active, and complete.","Comparing enum members to unrelated strings everywhere.","Use an enum in a task workflow."),
